/* ========== GCSE · LEVEL 1 · Year 9 refreshers (only if you need them) ========== */
U({id:'gp1-1',track:'gp',level:1,title:'Units and standard form',icon:'SI',color:'#378ADD',desc:'SI units and prefixes, converting units, standard form and significant figures.',lessons:[
LH('gp1-1-1','SI units and prefixes',8,'The units physicists use, and how kilo, mega and milli change them.',[
T(`<h3>SI units</h3><p>Scientists everywhere use the same units, called <b>SI units</b>. The ones you'll use most:</p><div class="tbl"><table class="wtable"><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Unit</th></tr><tr><td>length</td><td>metre (m)</td></tr><tr><td>mass</td><td>kilogram (kg)</td></tr><tr><td>time</td><td>second (s)</td></tr><tr><td>current</td><td>ampere (A)</td></tr><tr><td>temperature</td><td>kelvin (K) or °C</td></tr><tr><td>force</td><td>newton (N)</td></tr><tr><td>energy</td><td>joule (J)</td></tr><tr><td>power</td><td>watt (W)</td></tr></table></div><p>Physics equations only work if you put the numbers in using these units.</p>`),
W('matchPairs',{pairs:[['Force','newton (N)'],['Energy','joule (J)'],['Power','watt (W)'],['Potential difference','volt (V)'],['Resistance','ohm (Ω)'],['Mass','kilogram (kg)']]},`Match each quantity to its unit.`,`Learn these: they come up in every topic.`),
T(`<h3>Prefixes</h3><p>A prefix multiplies the unit:</p><ul><li><b>giga</b> (G) = 10⁹ = 1 000 000 000</li><li><b>mega</b> (M) = 10⁶ = 1 000 000</li><li><b>kilo</b> (k) = 10³ = 1000</li><li><b>centi</b> (c) = 10⁻² = 0.01</li><li><b>milli</b> (m) = 10⁻³ = 0.001</li><li><b>micro</b> (µ) = 10⁻⁶ &nbsp; <b>nano</b> (n) = 10⁻⁹</li></ul><p>So 3 kW = 3000 W, and 250 mA = 0.25 A.</p>`),
Q(`How many watts is 2 MW?`,[`2 000 000 W`,'2 000 W','200 000 W','20 000 000 W'],0,`Mega means a million.`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_units',need:6},`Convert the units. Get 6 right to master it.`,`Bigger unit to smaller: multiply. Smaller to bigger: divide.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Use SI units in equations: m, kg, s, A, N, J, W.</li><li>k = ×1000, M = ×1 000 000, m = ÷1000, c = ÷100.</li><li>Minutes to seconds: × 60. Hours to seconds: × 3600.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gp1-1-2','Standard form and significant figures',8,'Writing very big and very small numbers, and rounding answers sensibly.',[
RM(`<p>Prefixes: k = 10³, M = 10⁶, m = 10⁻³. SI units: m, kg, s.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Standard form</h3><p>Physics numbers can be enormous or tiny. Standard form writes them as <b>A × 10ⁿ</b>, where A is between 1 and 10.</p><ul><li>The speed of light: 300 000 000 m/s = 3 × 10⁸ m/s</li><li>The radius of an atom: 0.0000000001 m = 1 × 10⁻¹⁰ m</li></ul><p>Big numbers have a positive power; numbers less than 1 have a negative power.</p>`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_stdform',need:4},`Standard form. Get 4 right to master it.`,`Type A first, then the power.`),
T(`<h3>Significant figures</h3><p>Start counting at the first digit that isn't zero:</p><ul><li>0.004<b>56</b>7 to 2 s.f. is 0.0046</li><li>1234.5 to 2 s.f. is 1200</li></ul><p>In an exam, give your answer to the same number of significant figures as the data in the question (usually 2 or 3).</p>`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_sigfig',need:5},`Round to significant figures. Get 5 right to master it.`,`Zeros at the start don't count. Keep the place value.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Standard form: A × 10ⁿ, with A between 1 and 10.</li><li>Significant figures start at the first non-zero digit.</li><li>Match the s.f. of the data in the question.</li></ul>`)])
]});
U({id:'gp1-2',track:'gp',level:1,title:'Using equations',icon:'=',color:'#1D9E75',desc:'Substituting into physics equations step by step, and rearranging them.',lessons:[
LH('gp1-2-1','Substituting into equations',8,'A four-step method for every physics calculation.',[
T(`<h3>The four steps</h3><ol><li><b>Write the equation</b>.</li><li><b>Convert</b> every value into SI units.</li><li><b>Substitute</b> the numbers in.</li><li><b>Calculate</b>, and write the <b>unit</b>.</li></ol><p>Writing the equation first usually earns a mark even if you slip up later.</p>`),
W('stepCalc',{steps:[`A car travels 3 km in 2 minutes. Find its average speed.`,`1) Equation: speed = distance ÷ time`,`2) Units: 3 km = 3000 m; 2 min = 120 s`,`3) Substitute: speed = 3000 ÷ 120`,`4) Calculate: <b>25 m/s</b>`]},`A worked example.`,`Convert first, then calculate.`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_speed',need:4},`Speed, distance and time. Get 4 right to master it.`,`s = vt.`),
T(`<h3>Weight</h3><p>Weight is a force, caused by gravity pulling on a mass:</p><div class="eq">W = m g</div>${KY([['W','weight','N'],['m','mass','kg'],['g','gravitational field strength (9.8 on Earth)','N/kg']],'Mass is the amount of stuff (kg). Weight is a force (N), and it changes on the Moon.')}`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_weight',need:4},`Weight calculations. Get 4 right to master it.`,`W = mg, with g = 9.8 N/kg.`),
Q(`An astronaut goes to the Moon. What happens?`,[`Her mass stays the same, but her weight is less`,`Her mass and weight both fall`,`Her weight stays the same, but her mass falls`,`Nothing changes`],0,`The Moon's g is about 1.6 N/kg, but she's still made of the same stuff.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Write the equation, convert, substitute, calculate with units.</li><li>W = mg. Mass in kg, weight in N.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gp1-2-2','Rearranging equations',9,'Getting the letter you want on its own, so any equation works for you.',[
RM(`<p>Four steps: equation, convert, substitute, calculate. V = IR, ρ = m ÷ V.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Doing the same to both sides</h3><p>To get a letter on its own, undo what's being done to it, and do the same to both sides:</p><ul><li>If it's multiplied, divide. If it's divided, multiply.</li><li>If something's added, subtract. If it's squared, square-root.</li></ul>`),
W('stepCalc',{steps:[`Rearrange V = IR to make R the subject.`,`R is multiplied by I`,`Divide both sides by I: V ÷ I = R`,`So <b>R = V ÷ I</b>`]},`A worked example.`,`Undo the multiplication by dividing.`),
W('stepCalc',{steps:[`Rearrange E = ½mv² to make v the subject.`,`Multiply both sides by 2: 2E = mv²`,`Divide both sides by m: 2E ÷ m = v²`,`Square-root both sides: <b>v = √(2E ÷ m)</b>`]},`A harder one.`,`Undo the steps in reverse order.`),
Q(`Rearrange ρ = m ÷ V to make m the subject.`,[`m = ρV`,`m = ρ ÷ V`,`m = V ÷ ρ`,`m = ρ + V`],0,`m is divided by V, so multiply both sides by V.`),
Q(`Rearrange P = E ÷ t to make t the subject.`,[`t = E ÷ P`,`t = P ÷ E`,`t = PE`,`t = E − P`],0,`Multiply by t, then divide by P.`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:['g_vir','g_density'],need:5},`Calculations that need rearranging. Get 5 right to master it.`,`Rearrange first, then put the numbers in.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Do the same to both sides.</li><li>Undo multiplying by dividing, and squaring by square-rooting.</li><li>Rearrange first, then substitute.</li></ul>`)])
]});
U({id:'gp1-3',track:'gp',level:1,title:'Graphs and practical skills',icon:'📈',color:'#BA7517',desc:'Variables, accuracy and precision, anomalies and means, lines of best fit and gradients.',lessons:[
LH('gp1-3-1','Variables and good experiments',8,'The words examiners expect: variables, accuracy, precision, resolution and anomalies.',[
T(`<h3>Variables</h3><ul><li><b>Independent variable</b>: the one you change.</li><li><b>Dependent variable</b>: the one you measure.</li><li><b>Control variables</b>: everything you keep the same, so it's a fair test.</li></ul>`),
W('sortIt',{bins:['Independent','Dependent','Control'],items:[['The length of wire you choose',0],['The resistance you calculate from your readings',1],['The type of wire',2],['The temperature of the wire',2]]},`An experiment tests how the length of a wire affects its resistance. Sort the variables.`,`You change the length, measure the resistance, and keep everything else the same.`),
T(`<h3>Good data</h3><ul><li><b>Accurate</b>: close to the true value.</li><li><b>Precise</b>: repeat readings are close to each other.</li><li><b>Repeatable</b>: you get the same results if you do it again. <b>Reproducible</b>: someone else gets the same results.</li><li><b>Resolution</b>: the smallest change an instrument can show (1 mm on a ruler).</li></ul>`),
Q(`Three readings are 5.1, 5.2 and 5.1 s, but the true value is 6.0 s. The readings are…`,[`precise but not accurate`,`accurate but not precise`,`accurate and precise`,`neither`],0,`They're close together (precise) but far from the true value.`),
T(`<h3>Anomalies and means</h3><p>An <b>anomaly</b> is a result that doesn't fit the pattern. Leave it out, then find the <b>mean</b> of the rest: add them up and divide by how many there are.</p>`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_meanrep',need:3},`Spot the anomaly and find the mean. Get 3 right to master it.`,`Ignore the odd one out.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Independent: you change it. Dependent: you measure it. Control: keep it the same.</li><li>Accurate: near the true value. Precise: close together.</li><li>Leave out anomalies when you work out the mean.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gp1-3-2','Graphs and gradients',8,'Lines of best fit, proportional relationships, and finding a gradient.',[
RM(`<p>Independent variable on the x-axis (across), dependent on the y-axis (up).</p>`),
T(`<h3>Drawing a good graph</h3><ul><li>Label both axes with the quantity and unit.</li><li>Use a sensible scale that uses most of the grid.</li><li>Plot points with neat crosses.</li><li>Draw a single smooth <b>line of best fit</b>, straight or curved, with points evenly on both sides.</li></ul>`),
T(`<h3>Directly proportional</h3><p>If the graph is a <b>straight line through the origin</b>, the two quantities are <b>directly proportional</b>: double one and the other doubles.</p>`),
Q(`A graph is a straight line that does NOT go through the origin. Are the variables directly proportional?`,[`No: they're linearly related, but not proportional`,'Yes: any straight line shows direct proportion','Yes, as long as the line slopes upwards','You can\'t tell without knowing the units'],0,`Proportional needs the line to go through (0, 0) as well.`),
T(`<h3>Gradient</h3><div class="eq">gradient = change in y ÷ change in x</div><p>Pick two points far apart on the line. The gradient often means something: on a distance–time graph, it's the <b>speed</b>.</p>`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'gradpts',need:4},`Gradients from two points. Get 4 right to master it.`,`(y₂ − y₁) ÷ (x₂ − x₁).`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Label axes with units. Draw a smooth line of best fit.</li><li>Straight line through the origin: directly proportional.</li><li>Gradient = change in y ÷ change in x.</li></ul>`)])
]});
U({id:'gp1-4',track:'gp',level:1,title:'Forces and motion basics',icon:'→',color:'#D85A30',desc:'Contact and non-contact forces, balanced and unbalanced forces, speed, and distance–time graphs.',lessons:[
LH('gp1-4-1','Forces',8,'Pushes and pulls, contact and non-contact forces, and what balanced forces do.',[
T(`<h3>What a force is</h3><p>A force is a push or a pull, measured in <b>newtons</b> (N). Forces can change an object's speed, direction or shape.</p><ul><li><b>Contact forces</b>: friction, air resistance, tension, normal contact force.</li><li><b>Non-contact forces</b>: gravity (weight), magnetic, electrostatic.</li></ul>`),
W('sortIt',{bins:['Contact','Non-contact'],items:[['Friction',0],['Air resistance',0],['Tension in a rope',0],['Weight (gravity)',1],['Magnetic force',1],['Electrostatic force',1]]},`Contact or non-contact?`,`Non-contact forces act at a distance through a field.`),
T(`<h3>Balanced and unbalanced</h3><ul><li><b>Balanced</b> forces (they cancel out): the object stays still, or keeps moving at a <b>steady speed</b> in a straight line.</li><li><b>Unbalanced</b> forces: the object speeds up, slows down or changes direction.</li></ul><p>The overall force is the <b>resultant force</b>.</p>`),
Q(`A car drives at a steady 30 mph on a straight road. The forces on it are…`,[`balanced`,`unbalanced forwards`,`unbalanced backwards`,`zero: no forces act`],0,`Steady speed means the driving force equals the resistive forces.`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_resultant',need:3},`Resultant forces. Get 3 right to master it.`,`Same direction: add. Opposite directions: subtract.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Forces are in newtons. Contact or non-contact.</li><li>Balanced: still, or steady speed. Unbalanced: changing motion.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gp1-4-2','Speed and distance–time graphs',8,'Typical speeds, average speed, and reading distance–time graphs.',[
RM(`<p>speed = distance ÷ time, in m/s. The gradient of a distance–time graph is the speed.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Typical speeds</h3><ul><li>Walking: about 1.5 m/s</li><li>Cycling: about 6 m/s</li><li>A car: 13 to 30 m/s</li><li>Sound in air: about 330 m/s</li></ul><p>GCSE questions expect you to know these roughly.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Distance–time graphs</h3><ul><li>A <b>straight sloping line</b>: steady speed.</li><li>Steeper means <b>faster</b>.</li><li>A <b>flat line</b>: stationary (not moving).</li><li>Speed = the <b>gradient</b>.</li></ul>`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_dtgraph',need:4},`Distance–time graph questions. Get 4 right to master it.`,`Average speed uses total distance ÷ total time, rests included.`),
Q(`On a distance–time graph, the line gets steeper. What does this show?`,[`The object is speeding up`,'The object is slowing down','The object has stopped moving',`It is going backwards`],0,`Steeper gradient = greater speed.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Know typical speeds: walk 1.5, cycle 6, car 13 to 30, sound 330 m/s.</li><li>Distance–time: gradient = speed; flat = stopped.</li><li>Average speed = total distance ÷ total time.</li></ul>`)])
]});

U({id:'gc1-1',track:'gc',level:1,title:'Particles and states',icon:'∴',color:'#1D9E75',desc:'Solids, liquids and gases, changes of state, pure substances, dissolving and diffusion.',lessons:[
LH('gc1-1-1','Solids, liquids and gases',8,'The particle model and the names for every change of state.',[
T(`<h3>The particle model</h3><ul><li><b>Solid</b>: particles packed tightly in a regular pattern. They vibrate on the spot. Fixed shape.</li><li><b>Liquid</b>: particles close together but random, able to move past each other. Flows and takes the shape of its container.</li><li><b>Gas</b>: particles far apart, moving quickly in all directions. Fills its container and can be squashed.</li></ul>`),
W('matchPairs',{pairs:[['Solid → liquid','Melting'],['Liquid → gas','Boiling (or evaporating)'],['Gas → liquid','Condensing'],['Liquid → solid','Freezing'],['Solid → gas','Subliming']]},`Match each change of state to its name.`,`Melting and freezing happen at the same temperature, the melting point.`),
Q(`Why can a gas be squashed but a solid can't?`,[`Gas particles have big spaces between them`,'Gas particles are smaller than solid ones','Solid particles move faster and push back','Gases have no particles, only empty space'],0,`The particles can be pushed closer together.`),
Q(`What happens to the particles when a solid melts?`,[`They gain energy and can move past each other`,'They get bigger and push each other apart','They lose energy and slow down until they stop','They break apart into separate atoms and ions'],0,`Energy overcomes the forces holding them in place.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Solid: packed, vibrating. Liquid: close, moving past. Gas: far apart, fast.</li><li>Melting, boiling, condensing, freezing, subliming.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gc1-1-2','Pure substances and solutions',8,'What chemists mean by pure, dissolving, and diffusion.',[
RM(`<p>Solid particles vibrate in place; liquid particles move past each other; gas particles move fast and far apart.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Pure</h3><p>In everyday life, "pure" orange juice means nothing added. In chemistry, a <b>pure substance</b> is a single element or compound, with nothing else mixed in.</p><p>Pure substances melt and boil at a <b>specific temperature</b>. Mixtures melt over a <b>range</b> of temperatures. That's how you test purity.</p>`),
Q(`A sample melts between 72 °C and 78 °C. What does that tell you?`,[`It's a mixture (impure)`,'It\'s a pure compound','It\'s a pure element','It\'s a gas at room temp'],0,`Pure substances melt at one exact temperature.`),
T(`<h3>Dissolving</h3><ul><li>The <b>solute</b> dissolves in the <b>solvent</b> to make a <b>solution</b>.</li><li><b>Soluble</b>: it dissolves. <b>Insoluble</b>: it doesn't.</li><li>Mass is conserved: 100 g of water + 5 g of salt = 105 g of solution.</li></ul>`),
T(`<h3>Diffusion</h3><p>Particles spread out from where there are lots of them to where there are fewer, because they're moving randomly. That's why you can smell perfume across a room. Diffusion is fastest in gases and when it's warmer.</p>`),
Q(`Sugar dissolves in tea. Which is the solvent?`,[`The water in the tea`,'The sugar in the tea','The heat from the tea','The sweet solution'],0,`The solvent does the dissolving.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Pure: one substance only. Sharp melting point.</li><li>Solute + solvent → solution. Mass is conserved.</li><li>Diffusion: particles spread out by random motion.</li></ul>`)])
]});
U({id:'gc1-2',track:'gc',level:1,title:'Atoms and elements',icon:'Fe',color:'#7F77DD',desc:'Atoms, element symbols, molecules, counting atoms in formulas, and metals and non-metals.',lessons:[
LH('gc1-2-1','Atoms, symbols and formulas',8,'Element symbols, molecules, and reading a chemical formula.',[
T(`<h3>Atoms and elements</h3><p>Everything is made of tiny <b>atoms</b>. An <b>element</b> contains only one type of atom. Each element has a <b>symbol</b> of one or two letters; the first is always a capital.</p>`),
W('matchPairs',{pairs:[['Na','Sodium'],['K','Potassium'],['Fe','Iron'],['Cu','Copper'],['Cl','Chlorine'],['Ca','Calcium']]},`Match the symbols to the elements.`,`Some come from Latin: Na from natrium, K from kalium, Fe from ferrum, Cu from cuprum.`),
T(`<h3>Molecules and formulas</h3><p>A <b>molecule</b> is two or more atoms joined together. A <b>formula</b> shows which atoms, and how many:</p><ul><li>H₂O: 2 hydrogen atoms and 1 oxygen atom.</li><li>CO₂: 1 carbon and 2 oxygen.</li><li>Ca(OH)₂: the 2 multiplies everything in the bracket: 1 Ca, 2 O and 2 H.</li></ul>`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_count',need:5},`Count the atoms. Get 5 right to master it.`,`No small number means 1. A number after a bracket multiplies everything inside.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Element: one type of atom, with a symbol.</li><li>The small numbers in a formula count the atoms.</li><li>Brackets multiply everything inside.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gc1-2-2','Metals and non-metals',8,'How metals and non-metals behave differently, and where they are in the periodic table.',[
RM(`<p>An element has only one type of atom. Symbols: Na sodium, Fe iron, Cu copper.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Properties</h3><p><b>Metals</b> are usually shiny, good conductors of heat and electricity, <b>malleable</b> (can be hammered into shape), strong, and have high melting points (mercury is the odd one out).</p><p><b>Non-metals</b> are usually dull, poor conductors (graphite is the odd one out), brittle when solid, and many are gases.</p>`),
W('sortIt',{bins:['Metal','Non-metal'],items:[['Conducts electricity well',0],['Shiny',0],['Can be hammered into shape',0],['Dull',1],['Brittle',1],['Often a gas at room temperature',1]]},`Metal or non-metal?`,`Properties, not just names.`),
T(`<h3>Where they are</h3><p>In the periodic table, metals are on the <b>left</b> and in the middle; non-metals are on the <b>right</b>. A staircase line separates them.</p><p>Metal oxides are <b>bases</b> (they neutralise acids). Non-metal oxides, like CO₂ and SO₂, are often <b>acidic</b>.</p>`),
Q(`Sulfur dioxide dissolves in rainwater. What kind of solution does it make?`,[`Acidic`,`Alkaline`,`Neutral`,`Metallic`],0,`Non-metal oxides are acidic: this causes acid rain.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Metals: shiny, conductors, malleable. Non-metals: dull, insulators, brittle.</li><li>Metals on the left, non-metals on the right.</li><li>Metal oxides are basic; non-metal oxides are acidic.</li></ul>`)])
]});
U({id:'gc1-3',track:'gc',level:1,title:'Word and symbol equations',icon:'→',color:'#BA7517',desc:'Chemical and physical changes, signs of a reaction, word equations, and balancing symbol equations.',lessons:[
LH('gc1-3-1','Chemical reactions and word equations',8,'How to tell a chemical reaction from a physical change, and how to write word equations.',[
T(`<h3>Chemical or physical?</h3><ul><li>A <b>chemical change</b> makes new substances. It's usually hard to reverse.</li><li>A <b>physical change</b> (melting, dissolving) makes no new substance and is easy to reverse.</li></ul><p>Signs of a chemical reaction: <b>fizzing</b> (a gas is made), a <b>colour change</b>, a <b>temperature change</b>, or a <b>precipitate</b> (a solid forms in a solution).</p>`),
W('sortIt',{bins:['Chemical change','Physical change'],items:[['Burning wood',0],['Iron rusting',0],['Baking a cake',0],['Ice melting',1],['Salt dissolving in water',1],['Water boiling',1]]},`Chemical or physical change?`,`New substance made: chemical.`),
T(`<h3>Word equations</h3><p>Reactants on the left, products on the right, with an arrow meaning "react to make":</p><div class="eq">magnesium + oxygen → magnesium oxide</div><p>Common types: <b>combustion</b> (burning in oxygen), <b>thermal decomposition</b> (breaking down when heated), <b>oxidation</b> (gaining oxygen).</p>`),
Q(`Methane burns completely in oxygen. What are the products?`,[`Carbon dioxide and water`,'Carbon and hydrogen gas','Carbon monoxide only','Oxygen and water vapour'],0,`Hydrocarbon + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water.`),
Q(`Copper carbonate is heated and breaks down into copper oxide and carbon dioxide. What type of reaction is it?`,[`Thermal decomposition`,'Complete combustion','Acid neutralisation','Metal displacement'],0,`One substance breaks into two when heated.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Chemical change: new substance. Physical change: no new substance.</li><li>Signs: gas, colour change, temperature change, precipitate.</li><li>reactants → products.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gc1-3-2','Balancing symbol equations',9,'Why equations must balance, and a reliable method to balance them.',[
RM(`<p>A formula counts the atoms: H₂O is 2 H and 1 O. reactants → products.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Why balance?</h3><p>Atoms aren't made or destroyed in a reaction, just rearranged. So there must be the same number of each type of atom on both sides.</p><p>H₂ + O₂ → H₂O isn't balanced: 2 O on the left, only 1 on the right.</p>`),
T(`<h3>How to balance</h3><ol><li>Count each type of atom on both sides.</li><li>Put a <b>big number in front</b> of a formula to fix one element.</li><li>Recount and repeat until everything matches.</li></ol><p>Never change the small numbers: that would change the substance.</p>`),
W('stepCalc',{steps:[`Balance: H₂ + O₂ → H₂O`,`Count: left has 2 H and 2 O; right has 2 H and 1 O`,`Fix O: put 2 in front of H₂O → H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O. Now the right has 4 H and 2 O`,`Fix H: put 2 in front of H₂ → 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O`,`Check: 4 H and 2 O on each side. <b>Balanced</b>`]},`A worked example.`,`Fix one element at a time, then recount.`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_balance',need:4},`Balance the equations. Get 4 right to master it.`,`Type 1 where no number is needed.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Same number of each atom on both sides.</li><li>Change the big numbers in front, never the small ones.</li><li>Recount after every change.</li></ul>`)])
]});
U({id:'gc1-4',track:'gc',level:1,title:'Acids and reactions',icon:'pH',color:'#D4537E',desc:'Acids, alkalis, indicators and pH, neutralisation, acids with metals, and tests for gases.',lessons:[
LH('gc1-4-1','Acids, alkalis and pH',8,'The pH scale, indicators, and neutralisation.',[
T(`<h3>The pH scale</h3><p>The pH scale runs from 0 to 14:</p><ul><li>Below 7: <b>acidic</b> (lemon juice, vinegar, stomach acid).</li><li>7: <b>neutral</b> (pure water).</li><li>Above 7: <b>alkaline</b> (soap, bleach, oven cleaner).</li></ul><p>Strong acids and alkalis are corrosive.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Indicators</h3><ul><li><b>Litmus</b>: red in acid, blue in alkali.</li><li><b>Universal indicator</b>: red (strong acid) → orange → yellow → green (neutral) → blue → purple (strong alkali). It shows the pH.</li></ul>`),
Q(`Universal indicator turns purple. The solution is…`,[`strongly alkaline`,`strongly acidic`,`neutral`,`weakly acidic`],0,`Purple is around pH 13 to 14.`),
T(`<h3>Neutralisation</h3><p>An acid and an alkali cancel each other out:</p><div class="eq">acid + alkali → salt + water</div><p>Indigestion tablets neutralise extra stomach acid.</p>`),
Q(`What is made when hydrochloric acid neutralises sodium hydroxide?`,[`Sodium chloride and water`,`Sodium and chlorine`,`Hydrogen and oxygen`,`Sodium hydroxide and water`],0,`A salt (sodium chloride) and water.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>pH below 7 acid, 7 neutral, above 7 alkali.</li><li>Universal indicator shows the pH by colour.</li><li>acid + alkali → salt + water.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gc1-4-2','Acids, metals and gas tests',8,'What happens when metals meet acids, and the three gas tests you must know.',[
RM(`<p>acid + alkali → salt + water. pH below 7 is acidic.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Metals and acids</h3><div class="eq">metal + acid → salt + hydrogen</div><p>More reactive metals fizz faster. Magnesium fizzes quickly; copper doesn't react at all.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Gas tests</h3><ul><li><b>Hydrogen</b>: a lit splint gives a <b>squeaky pop</b>.</li><li><b>Oxygen</b>: <b>relights a glowing splint</b>.</li><li><b>Carbon dioxide</b>: turns <b>limewater cloudy</b> (milky).</li></ul>`),
W('matchPairs',{pairs:[['Hydrogen','Squeaky pop with a lit splint'],['Oxygen','Relights a glowing splint'],['Carbon dioxide','Turns limewater cloudy']]},`Match each gas to its test.`,`These three come up in GCSE every year.`),
Q(`Zinc is added to an acid. It fizzes. How would you test the gas?`,[`Hold a lit splint near it: a squeaky pop means hydrogen`,'Bubble it through limewater: if it turns cloudy, it\'s hydrogen','Hold a glowing splint in it: if it relights, it\'s hydrogen','Hold damp litmus paper in it: if it bleaches, it\'s hydrogen'],0,`Metal + acid makes hydrogen.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>metal + acid → salt + hydrogen.</li><li>Hydrogen: squeaky pop. Oxygen: relights glowing splint. CO₂: limewater cloudy.</li></ul>`)])
]});

U({id:'gm1-1',track:'gm',level:1,title:'Number skills',icon:'±',color:'#7F77DD',desc:'Negative numbers, the order of operations, factors, multiples, primes, HCF and LCM.',lessons:[
LH('gm1-1-1','Negatives and order of operations',8,'Working with negative numbers, and the order to do calculations in.',[
T(`<h3>Negative numbers</h3><ul><li>Adding a negative is the same as subtracting: 5 + (−3) = 2.</li><li>Subtracting a negative is the same as adding: 5 − (−3) = 8.</li><li>Multiplying or dividing: <b>same signs give positive</b>, <b>different signs give negative</b>. (−4) × (−3) = 12; (−4) × 3 = −12.</li></ul>`),
Q(`What is −6 − (−10)?`,[`4`,`−16`,`16`,`−4`],0,`−6 + 10 = 4.`),
T(`<h3>Order of operations</h3><p><b>B</b>rackets, <b>I</b>ndices (powers), <b>D</b>ivision and <b>M</b>ultiplication, <b>A</b>ddition and <b>S</b>ubtraction (BIDMAS).</p><div class="eq">3 + 4 × 2 = 3 + 8 = 11, not 14</div>`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_bidmas',need:6},`Order of operations. Get 6 right to master it.`,`Powers before multiplying; multiplying before adding.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Same signs: positive. Different signs: negative.</li><li>BIDMAS: brackets, powers, × and ÷, + and −.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gm1-1-2','Factors, multiples and primes',8,'Prime factors, highest common factor and lowest common multiple.',[
RM(`<p>A factor divides exactly into a number. A multiple is in its times table.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Primes and prime factors</h3><p>A <b>prime</b> has exactly two factors: 1 and itself (2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13…). 1 is not prime.</p><p>Every number can be written as a <b>product of prime factors</b>. Use a factor tree:</p><div class="eq">60 = 2 × 2 × 3 × 5 = 2² × 3 × 5</div>`),
Q(`Write 36 as a product of prime factors.`,[`2² × 3²`,`4 × 9`,`2 × 3 × 6`,`6²`],0,`36 = 2 × 2 × 3 × 3. 4 and 9 aren't prime.`),
T(`<h3>HCF and LCM</h3><ul><li><b>HCF</b> (highest common factor): the biggest number that divides into both. Multiply the primes they share.</li><li><b>LCM</b> (lowest common multiple): the smallest number both divide into. Use every prime the most times it appears.</li></ul><p>12 = 2² × 3 and 18 = 2 × 3²: HCF = 2 × 3 = 6; LCM = 2² × 3² = 36.</p>`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_hcf',need:5},`HCF and LCM. Get 5 right to master it.`,`HCF: shared primes. LCM: every prime, the most times.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Primes have exactly two factors. 1 isn't prime.</li><li>Factor trees give the product of primes.</li><li>HCF: shared primes. LCM: all primes, most times each.</li></ul>`)])
]});
U({id:'gm1-2',track:'gm',level:1,title:'Algebra basics',icon:'a',color:'#1D9E75',desc:'Collecting like terms, substitution, expanding a single bracket, solving simple equations and linear sequences.',lessons:[
LH('gm1-2-1','Simplifying and substituting',8,'Collecting like terms, and putting numbers into expressions.',[
T(`<h3>Algebra notation</h3><ul><li>3x means 3 × x. xy means x × y.</li><li>x² means x × x. 2x² means 2 × x × x, not (2x)².</li></ul><h3>Like terms</h3><p>Like terms have exactly the same letters and powers. Add or subtract their numbers: 5a + 3b − 2a + b = 3a + 4b.</p>`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_collect',need:5},`Collect like terms. Get 5 right to master it.`,`x terms together, y terms together.`),
T(`<h3>Substitution</h3><p>Replace each letter with its number, then use BIDMAS. Put negative numbers in brackets so the signs come out right.</p>`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_subst',need:5},`Substitution. Get 5 right to master it.`,`Brackets around negatives, then BIDMAS.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Like terms: same letters and powers.</li><li>Substitute with brackets around negatives.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gm1-2-2','Brackets, equations and sequences',9,'Expanding a single bracket, solving simple equations, and the nth term of a linear sequence.',[
RM(`<p>Collect like terms. Substitute with brackets around negatives.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Expanding one bracket</h3><p>Multiply everything inside by the term outside:</p><div class="eq">4(2x − 3) = 8x − 12 &nbsp;&nbsp; −2(x + 5) = −2x − 10</div>`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_expand1',need:5},`Expand the brackets. Get 5 right to master it.`,`Watch the signs when the outside number is negative.`),
T(`<h3>Solving equations</h3><p>Do the same to both sides to get x on its own:</p><div class="eq">3x + 5 = 20 → 3x = 15 → x = 5</div>`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_solvelin',need:4},`Solve the equations. Get 4 right to master it.`,`Collect the x terms on one side first.`),
T(`<h3>Linear sequences</h3><p>In 5, 8, 11, 14, … the terms go up by 3, so the nth term starts with <b>3n</b>. 3n gives 3, 6, 9… so add 2: nth term = <b>3n + 2</b>.</p>`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_nth',need:4},`nth terms. Get 4 right to master it.`,`The difference is the number in front of n.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Expand: multiply every term inside.</li><li>Solve: same to both sides.</li><li>nth term: difference × n, then adjust.</li></ul>`)])
]});
U({id:'gm1-3',track:'gm',level:1,title:'Fractions, decimals and percentages',icon:'½',color:'#BA7517',desc:'Adding, multiplying and dividing fractions, fractions of amounts, and converting between fractions, decimals and percentages.',lessons:[
LH('gm1-3-1','Fractions',9,'Adding, multiplying and dividing fractions, and finding a fraction of an amount.',[
T(`<h3>Adding and subtracting</h3><p>Make the <b>denominators</b> (bottoms) the same first, then add or subtract the tops:</p><div class="eq">1/3 + 1/4 = 4/12 + 3/12 = 7/12</div>`),
T(`<h3>Multiplying and dividing</h3><ul><li>Multiply: tops × tops, bottoms × bottoms. 2/3 × 3/5 = 6/15 = 2/5.</li><li>Divide: <b>flip the second fraction</b> and multiply. 2/3 ÷ 4/5 = 2/3 × 5/4 = 10/12 = 5/6.</li></ul><p>Always simplify at the end.</p>`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_frac',need:6},`Fraction calculations. Get 6 right to master it.`,`Add: same bottoms. Multiply: straight across. Divide: flip and multiply.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Add or subtract: common denominator.</li><li>Multiply: straight across. Divide: flip the second, then multiply.</li><li>Fraction of an amount: ÷ bottom × top.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gm1-3-2','Decimals and percentages',8,'Converting between fractions, decimals and percentages, and simple percentages.',[
RM(`<p>A fraction is a division: 3/4 means 3 ÷ 4.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Converting</h3><ul><li>Fraction → decimal: divide the top by the bottom. 3/8 = 0.375.</li><li>Decimal → percentage: × 100. 0.375 = 37.5%.</li><li>Percentage → fraction: put it over 100 and simplify. 35% = 35/100 = 7/20.</li></ul>`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_fdp',need:6},`Fractions, decimals and percentages. Get 6 right to master it.`,`Decimal × 100 = percentage.`),
T(`<h3>Percentages of amounts</h3><p>Turn the percentage into a decimal and multiply: 15% of £80 = 0.15 × 80 = £12.</p>`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_pct',need:4},`Percentage questions. Get 4 right to master it.`,`Use multipliers.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Fraction → decimal: divide. Decimal → %: × 100.</li><li>% → fraction: over 100, then simplify.</li></ul>`)])
]});
U({id:'gm1-4',track:'gm',level:1,title:'Shapes and angles',icon:'∠',color:'#D85A30',desc:'Basic angle rules, triangles, area and perimeter of 2D shapes.',lessons:[
LH('gm1-4-1','Angle rules',8,'Angles on a line, around a point, in triangles and in isosceles triangles.',[
T(`<h3>The basic rules</h3><ul><li>Angles on a <b>straight line</b> add up to <b>180°</b>.</li><li>Angles <b>around a point</b> add up to <b>360°</b>.</li><li>Angles in a <b>triangle</b> add up to <b>180°</b>.</li><li>Angles in a <b>quadrilateral</b> add up to <b>360°</b>.</li><li><b>Vertically opposite</b> angles are equal.</li><li>An <b>isosceles</b> triangle has two equal sides and two equal base angles.</li></ul>`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_angle1',need:6},`Angle rules. Get 6 right to master it.`,`Write the rule you used each time, like you would in an exam.`),
Q(`Two angles in a quadrilateral are 90° and 110°. The other two are equal. What is each one?`,[`80°`,`160°`,`70°`,`100°`],0,`360 − 90 − 110 = 160, then ÷ 2 = 80°.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Line 180°, point 360°, triangle 180°, quadrilateral 360°.</li><li>Vertically opposite angles are equal.</li><li>Isosceles: two equal base angles.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gm1-4-2','Area and perimeter',8,'Perimeter, and the area of rectangles, triangles, parallelograms and trapeziums.',[
RM(`<p>Perimeter is the distance around a shape. Area is the space inside, in square units.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Area formulas</h3><ul><li>Rectangle: length × width</li><li>Triangle: ½ × base × height</li><li>Parallelogram: base × perpendicular height</li><li>Trapezium: ½(a + b) × h, where a and b are the parallel sides</li></ul><p>The height must be <b>perpendicular</b> (at right angles) to the base.</p>`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_area1',need:6},`Areas of 2D shapes. Get 6 right to master it.`,`Use the perpendicular height, not the slanted side.`),
Q(`A rectangle is 8 cm by 5 cm. What is its perimeter?`,[`26 cm`,`40 cm`,`13 cm`,`80 cm`],0,`8 + 5 + 8 + 5 = 26 cm.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Rectangle lw, triangle ½bh, parallelogram bh, trapezium ½(a + b)h.</li><li>Always use the perpendicular height.</li></ul>`)])
]});

